Body:
Does the acp-sdk support passing mcpServers in the session: [ACP#Session Setup](https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/session-setup#http-transport), [NewSessionRequest](https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/typescript-sdk/blob/main/src/schema/types.gen.ts#L1623)

In the source code opencode-agent.ts, the newSession function has a defined mcpServers parameter:[agent.ts-mcpServers](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/c6262f9d4002d86a1f1795c306aa329d45361d12/packages/opencode/src/acp/agent.ts#L568) @haoqixu 

But during the actual chat conversation process, are the mcpServers defined in the session state not being used?

I test in this way
```bash
curl -X POST 'http://localhost:14096/session' \
-H 'x-opencode-directory: /tmp' \
-H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
-d '{
        "mcpServers": [
            {
                "type": "http",
                "name": "custom-mcp-server",
                "url": "http://xxx/mcp/sse",
                "headers": [
                    {
                        "name": "Authorization",
                        "value": "Bearer wMU+xxx="
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Content-Type",
                        "value": "application/json"
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
    ' | jq
```

does opencode support  for per-session MCP servers?

@jlongster thanks for the review!

> I fixed the read case with the assumption in mind that opencode has always generated and cached the project in in the local project's .git dir. You're right that we should the write case too, but it's a rare edge case right?

Correct, the initial issue is with worktrees created outside opencode ([worktrunk](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk) is fantastic btw.) I'm not sure how rare of an edge case this is -- I've been using worktrees like this for years 😅. I'll create one for every PR and run opencode in there.

@OpeOginni I'm running the command with the hostname parameter you mentioned, e.g.

`cd "/projects/$PROJECT_NAME" && exec opencode serve --port 4321 --hostname 0.0.0.0`

I can also connect to it. However, I would expect that session, when connected to, to start in `/projects/$PROJECT_NAME` and not in `/` . The latter is the case and I can't choose the right folder in the macos Desktop app
